## Advantages of React Hooks

## 1. Reusable Stateful Logic

Before hooks, sharing stateful logic required HOCs or render props — both add wrapper components to the tree. Custom hooks extract logic with zero nesting:

```tsx
// Reusable across any component
function useWindowSize() {
  const [size, setSize] = useState({ width: 0, height: 0 });
  useEffect(() => {
    const handler = () => setSize({ width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight });
    window.addEventListener('resize', handler);
    handler();
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', handler);
  }, []);
  return size;
}
```

## 2. Co-located Related Logic

Class components split one concern across multiple lifecycle methods. Hooks keep related code together:

```tsx
// Class: subscribe in componentDidMount, unsubscribe in componentWillUnmount
// Hooks: same effect handles both
useEffect(() => {
  const sub = store.subscribe(handler);
  return () => sub.unsubscribe(); // cleanup lives next to setup
}, []);
```

## 3. No More `this`

Class components require binding event handlers and understanding `this` context. Hooks use plain closures.

## 4. Easier Testing

Custom hooks are plain functions — testable with `renderHook` from React Testing Library without mounting a full component.

## 5. Smaller Bundle

Functional components with hooks produce less compiled code than class components.

## useEffect vs Lifecycle Methods

## Mapping

```tsx
// componentDidMount
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('mounted');
}, []); // empty array = run once

// componentDidUpdate (specific deps)
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('userId changed:', userId);
}, [userId]);

// componentWillUnmount
useEffect(() => {
  const sub = subscribe();
  return () => sub.unsubscribe(); // ← cleanup = componentWillUnmount
}, []);

// componentDidMount + componentDidUpdate combined
useEffect(() => {
  document.title = `Count: ${count}`;
}); // no array = after every render
```

## What useEffect Can't Replace

| Class lifecycle | Hook equivalent |
|---|---|
| `componentDidMount` | `useEffect(() => {}, [])` |
| `componentDidUpdate` | `useEffect(() => {}, [dep])` |
| `componentWillUnmount` | cleanup in `useEffect` return |
| `getSnapshotBeforeUpdate` | No direct equivalent |
| `shouldComponentUpdate` | `React.memo` + `useMemo` |
| `componentDidCatch` | Error Boundary class component (still required) |

## useLayoutEffect

For synchronous DOM reads/mutations after paint (equivalent to the synchronous part of `componentDidMount`):

```tsx
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  // Runs synchronously after DOM mutations, before browser paint
  const rect = ref.current?.getBoundingClientRect();
  setTooltipPosition(rect);
}, [visible]);
```

## React Batching

## Before React 18

Only state updates inside React event handlers were batched:

```tsx
// React event handler — 1 re-render (batched)
function handleClick() {
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setFlag(f => !f);
}

// setTimeout — 2 re-renders (NOT batched in React 17)
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount(c => c + 1); // render 1
  setFlag(f => !f);     // render 2
}, 1000);
```

## React 18 — Automatic Batching

All updates are batched regardless of origin:

```tsx
// React 18 — all batched to 1 re-render
setTimeout(() => {
  setCount(c => c + 1);
  setFlag(f => !f);
}, 1000);

fetch('/api').then(() => {
  setData(d);    // batched
  setLoading(false); // batched → 1 render
});
```

## Opting Out with flushSync

When you need a state update to flush immediately (e.g., to measure DOM before the next update):

```tsx
import { flushSync } from 'react-dom';

flushSync(() => setCount(c => c + 1)); // renders synchronously
console.log(ref.current?.textContent); // sees updated DOM
flushSync(() => setFlag(f => !f));
```

## Advantages

- Fewer renders → better performance.
- Consistent UI — no intermediate states where only some updates have been applied.
- Predictable behaviour across all async contexts in React 18.

## Custom Hooks vs Normal Functions

## The Only Difference: naming + hook calls

```tsx
// Normal function — cannot call hooks
function getWindowSize() {
  // useState(0) here would throw: Rules of Hooks violation
  return { width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight };
}

// Custom hook — can call hooks
function useWindowSize() {
  const [size, setSize] = useState({ width: 0, height: 0 });
  useEffect(() => {
    const update = () => setSize({ width: window.innerWidth, height: window.innerHeight });
    window.addEventListener('resize', update);
    update();
    return () => window.removeEventListener('resize', update);
  }, []);
  return size;
}
```

## Isolated State

Each component that calls the same custom hook gets its **own isolated state**. The hook is not a singleton:

```tsx
function PageA() {
  const { width } = useWindowSize(); // independent instance
}
function PageB() {
  const { width } = useWindowSize(); // independent instance
}
```

## Common Patterns

```tsx
// Data fetching
function useUser(id: string) {
  const [data, setData] = useState<User | null>(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  useEffect(() => {
    fetchUser(id).then(u => { setData(u); setLoading(false); });
  }, [id]);
  return { data, loading };
}

// Form
function useField(initial = '') {
  const [value, setValue] = useState(initial);
  return { value, onChange: (e: React.ChangeEvent<HTMLInputElement>) => setValue(e.target.value) };
}
```

## Hook State Isolation

## Each Call = Independent State

```tsx
function useCounter(initial = 0) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(initial);
  return { count, increment: () => setCount(c => c + 1) };
}

function ComponentA() {
  const { count } = useCounter(); // own state: 0
}
function ComponentB() {
  const { count } = useCounter(); // own state: 0 — completely separate
}
```

Clicking increment in A does not affect B.

## Sharing State Options

### 1. Lift state up

```tsx
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return (
    <>
      <ChildA count={count} />
      <ChildB count={count} onChange={setCount} />
    </>
  );
}
```

### 2. Context

```tsx
const CountContext = createContext(0);

function Provider({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  return <CountContext.Provider value={{ count, setCount }}>{children}</CountContext.Provider>;
}
```

### 3. External store (Zustand)

```tsx
const useStore = create((set) => ({ count: 0, inc: () => set(s => ({ count: s.count + 1 })) }));
// Both components subscribe to the same store — shared state
```

## useState Async Behaviour

## The Stale Closure Problem

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

function handleClick() {
  setCount(count + 1);
  console.log(count); // still 0 — state hasn't updated yet

  setCount(count + 1); // also sets to 1, not 2!
  setCount(count + 1); // still sets to 1!
}
```

## Solution: Functional Updates

```tsx
function handleClick() {
  setCount(prev => prev + 1); // 0 → 1
  setCount(prev => prev + 1); // 1 → 2
  setCount(prev => prev + 1); // 2 → 3
  // All three updaters queue, result: 3
}
```

## Reading Updated State

If you need the new value in the same handler, derive it first:

```tsx
const newCount = count + 1;
setCount(newCount);
trackEvent('count_changed', { value: newCount }); // use derived value
```

## useEffect to React to State Changes

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  console.log('count updated:', count); // runs after re-render with new value
}, [count]);
```

## React 18 Batching

Multiple `setState` calls in the same event handler (or async callback in React 18) are batched into a single render. This is intentional — it prevents unnecessary intermediate renders.

## Updating Nested State with useState

## Shallow Merge — Always Spread

```tsx
const [form, setForm] = useState({ name: '', email: '', address: { city: '', zip: '' } });

// Update top-level field
setForm(prev => ({ ...prev, name: 'Aaron' }));

// Update nested field — must spread nested object too
setForm(prev => ({
  ...prev,
  address: { ...prev.address, city: 'London' },
}));
```

## Why Not Mutate Directly?

```tsx
// WRONG — same object reference, React bails out of re-render
form.address.city = 'London';
setForm(form);
```

React uses reference equality to detect changes. Mutating and re-setting the same object doesn't trigger a re-render.

## useReducer for Complex Shapes

```tsx
type Action =
  | { type: 'SET_NAME'; payload: string }
  | { type: 'SET_CITY'; payload: string };

function reducer(state: FormState, action: Action): FormState {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'SET_CITY':
      return { ...state, address: { ...state.address, city: action.payload } };
  }
}
```

## Immer (cleanest for deep nesting)

```tsx
import { useImmer } from 'use-immer';

const [form, updateForm] = useImmer({ name: '', address: { city: '' } });

// Mutating syntax — Immer handles immutability under the hood
updateForm(draft => { draft.address.city = 'London'; });
```
